WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
(Intro SFX)

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.970
- Okay, what exactly is happening
with the iPhone's camera?

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.060
Like we've done years of
blind smartphone camera tests

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.010
in bracket format and the iPhone,

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.720
supposedly one of the premium cameras

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.250
in the entire smartphone industry,

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.530
consistently loses in the first round.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.680
Then we do a scientific
version with 20 million+ votes

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.667
and it finishes in the middle of the pack.

00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:25.620
"And yet, Marques, you named
it the fourth-time running

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.620
best overall smartphone
camera system in 2022

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.090
and gave it a trophy.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.140
What's up with that?"

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.550
A concerning amount of people have started

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.590
to notice that the iPhones camera

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.920
feels like it's taken
a few steps back lately

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.600
and I agree with them.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.502
I think we should take
a closer look at this.

00:00:42.502 --> 00:00:43.335
(relaxed music)

00:00:43.335 --> 00:00:46.710
So first of all, cameras
have come a really long way

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.750
to the point where smartphone cameras

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.880
aren't just cameras anymore.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.120
See, back in the day, a
camera was basically a sensor

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.100
that would travel around
covered all the time

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.420
and when you wanted to take a photo,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:58.950
you would expose that sensitive bit

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.120
to the environment around it

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.190
and it would collect
the light and close it.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.900
Then the photo would be a representation

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.870
of how much light hit
each part of the sensor.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.060
The better the sensor, the
better an image you could get,

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.550
the more light information, super simple.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.540
These days though,

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.760
it's turned into a whole
computational event.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.920
Your smartphone sensor is
sampling the environment,

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.200
not once, but often several times

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.510
in rapid succession at different speeds.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.130
It's taking that light information,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.870
merging exposures together.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:29.870
It's doing tone mapping, noise
reduction, HDR processing

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.190
and putting it all together

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.250
into what it thinks will
be the best looking image.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.083
This, of course,

00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:38.880
is a very different
definition of a picture.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.820
So now it's not just about
having the best sensor

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.070
that gathers the most light information,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.410
it's at the point where software

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.700
makes a much bigger difference

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.920
to the way the image looks
at the end of the day

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:50.970
than anything else.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:51.803
Like next time you watch

00:01:51.803 --> 00:01:54.240
a smartphone reveal event, for example,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.520
keep an eye on all the new
additions that get made

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.040
and just how many of
them are pure software.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.410
So Google basically struck
gold when they first

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.960
started using the IMX363 sensor

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.450
way back in the day with
the Pixel 3's camera

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.690
because they got their software
tuning with it just right

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.520
and it was an instant smash hit.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.040
So they kept using that great camera combo

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.570
in every Pixel since then.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.530
The 3, the 3a, the 4, the 4a,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.440
the 5, the 5a, and even the Pixel 6a.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.630
So year after year of new phones,

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.150
same sensor, same software tuning combo

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:28.650
because it just worked.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.970
If it ain't broke, don't fix it.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.190
So when you saw the Pixel 6a

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.880
win December's scientific
blind smartphone camera test,

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.090
what you saw was a four-year-old sensor

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:42.390
and software tuning combo
that is still so good

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.520
that in a postage-stamp-sized comparison

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.170
of compressed side-by-side images

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.490
where you can't really judge sharpness

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.900
or depth of field too much,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.120
basically just appreciating the basics,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.880
this combo absolutely nailed the basics

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:54.720
better than anyone else.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.210
Now, when the Pixel 6
came along, stay with me,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.570
Google finally updated their
design and their branding

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.780
and they finally changed to a new sensor

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.070
with this new camera system.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.410
So they go from the
tried-and-true 12 megapixel

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.920
to this massive new 50 megapixel sensor

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.070
and it kind of threw a wrench into things.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:18.150
- So it looks to me that the
Pixel is over sharpening.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.520
I think the one on the
left looks too crunchy.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.320
- The camera on the
Pixel 6 does have a habit

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.480
of making things just look HDR-y.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.520
I dunno if there's really
a technical term for that.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.840
- [Dan] And if you look at all the photos,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.150
it's clear the Pixel is
still doing Pixel things.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.330
- I think Google's still running all

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.060
of their camera algorithms at 11,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.800
like when they don't need to anymore.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.170
- Right now, new phones
with much bigger sensors

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:43.650
are still processing like
their smaller older ones.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.030
- The basic principle is:

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.790
they were doing all this
processing with the old sensors

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.320
as if they were not getting a lot of light

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.510
and then suddenly they had
this massive new sensor

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.790
which is getting way
more light information

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.550
but they were still running
all of this processing.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.710
They would still do high-sensitivity stuff

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.030
and then they'd do noise reduction

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.800
because if you have high sensitivity,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:02.640
you need noise reduction.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.230
But then since you're
doing noise reduction,

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.730
you need to do sharpening on top of that

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:06.563
to make up for it

00:04:06.563 --> 00:04:09.600
and just overall you're
doing way too much.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.630
And so the photos are
literally overprocessed.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:13.980
So this fancy new phone would come out

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:15.420
with a new camera system,

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:16.890
but you could argue, legitimately,

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.550
that the older Pixel still
took better looking photos.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:22.620
So Google had to work really
hard at the drawing board

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.440
and make some adjustments and
some updates to the software

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.060
to dial in this new sensor.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.700
It took a while, but
now with the Pixel 7 out

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.210
a full year later with the
same huge 50 megapixel sensor,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.500
they're back on track.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.820
And hey would you look at that,

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.150
Pixel 7 right behind the Pixel
6a in the blind camera test.

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:41.430
So when I see iPhone 14 Pro photos

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.750
looking a little inconsistent

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.810
and a little overprocessed right now,

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.100
I actually see a lot of the same stuff

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.320
that Google just went
through with the Pixel.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.170
Because the iPhone story is
kind of along the same lines,

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:54.510
they used a small 12 megapixel sensor

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:56.430
for years and years and years.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.380
Then the 13 Pro sensor got a little bigger

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.150
but this year, the iPhone 14 Pro

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.770
is the first time they're bumping up

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.670
to this dramatically
larger 48 megapixel sensor.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:06.690
And so guess what?

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:08.310
Some iPhone photos this year

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.010
are looking a little too processed

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.650
and it's nothing extreme, but it's real

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:14.790
and they will have to work on this.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.230
I suspect that by the time we get

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:20.220
to iPhone 15 Pro, you know, a year later,

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.740
they'll have some new software
stuff they're working on.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:26.310
And I bet there's one new
word they use on stage.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.350
You know, we finally have Deep Fusion

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.850
and pixel-binning and all this stuff,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.100
I bet there's one new word they use

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:34.860
to explain some software
improvement with the camera.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.630
But anyway, I think this
will continue improving

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:37.950
with software updates over time

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.870
and they'll continue to get it dialed

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.170
and I think it'll be fine.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.540
But that's only half my theory.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.760
This does not explain why

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.490
all the previous 12 megapixel iPhones

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:52.920
also all lost in the first round

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.540
in all those other bracket style tests.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:55.590
And this is a separate issue

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.320
that I'm actually a
little more curious about

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.360
because as you might recall,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:05.070
all of our testing photos
have been photos of me.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.630
Now, this was on purpose, right?

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.100
Like we specifically designed the tests

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.020
to have as many potential factors

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.490
to judge a photo as possible.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.230
Like if it was just a
picture of this figurine

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.760
in front of a white wall,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.580
the winner would probably just
be whichever one's brighter,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.250
maybe whichever one has a
better gold color, basically.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.540
But then if we take the figurine

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.460
with some falloff in the background

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.910
now we're judging both
color and background blur.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.320
Maybe you add a sky to the background,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.410
now you're also testing
dynamic range and HDR.

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.690
So yeah, with our latest
photo, it's a lot.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.040
It's two different skin tones.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.870
It's two different colored shirts.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.150
It's some textures for sharpness,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.220
the sky back there for a dynamic range,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.720
short-range falloff on the left,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:43.950
long-range falloff on the right.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.510
I mean with all these factors,

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.160
whichever one people pick as a winner

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.370
ideally is closer to
the best overall photo.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:52.050
I also wanted the
pictures to be of a human

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:52.920
just because I feel like

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.410
most of the important
pictures that people take,

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.170
most often, that they care
about are of other humans.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.100
But as it turns out,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.250
using my own face as a subject for these

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.920
revealed a lot about how
different smartphones

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.820
handle taking a picture of a human face.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.500
Because as I've already mentioned,

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:14.820
these smartphone cameras
are so much software now

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.360
that the photo that you get
when you hit that shutter button

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.820
isn't so much reality

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:25.230
as much as it is this
computer's best interpretation

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:29.340
of what it thinks you
want reality to look like.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.370
And each company goes to a different level

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.360
of making different choices
and different optimizations

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.580
to change their pictures
up to look different ways.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.590
They used to actually be a
little more transparent about it.

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:42.480
There are phones that
would literally identify

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.190
when you're taking a landscape photo

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.160
and they'd pump up any greens
they can find of the grass

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.440
or they'd identify any
picture with a sky in it

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.660
and pump up the blues
to make it look nicer.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.420
I did a whole video on
smartphone cameras versus reality

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.500
that I'll link below the Like button

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:56.400
if you wanna check it out.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.720
But the point is, when you
snap that photo on your phone,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.730
you're not necessarily
getting back a capture

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.530
of what was really in front of you.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.110
They're really bending it in many ways.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.820
The iPhone's thing is
when you take a photo

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:16.820
it likes to identify faces
and evenly light them.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:19.950
It tries every time.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.900
And so this feels like a
pretty innocent thing, right?

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.377
Like if you ask people normally,

00:08:23.377 --> 00:08:25.680
"What do you think should
look good in a photo?"

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.350
And you say, "Oh, I'll evenly
light all the faces in it."

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.940
That sounds fine, right?

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.560
And a lot of time it looks fine

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.690
but it's a subtle thing like in a photo

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.510
where you can see the light is
coming from one side clearly,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.460
where you can see from the Pixel's camera,

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:41.340
there's a shadow on the
right side of the face.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:42.450
With the iPhone though,

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:43.800
it's almost like someone walked up

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.750
and added a little bounce fill, (chuckles)

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:48.390
just a really nice little
subtle bounce fill.

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:50.130
But sometimes it looks a little off.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.560
Like look, this is the
low-light photo test we did

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.180
from our blind camera test.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.220
On the left is the Pixel 7 again,

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:57.840
which looks like all the other top dogs.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.450
And on the right is the iPhone 14 Pro

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:01.740
that finished in the middle of the pack.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.440
It might be hard at first
to see why it looks so weird

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.980
but look at how they
completely removed the shadow

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:09.780
from half of my face.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.280
I am clearly being lit

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.410
from a source that's to the side of me,

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:15.960
and that's part of reality.

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.170
But in the iPhone's
reality, you cannot tell,

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.150
at least from my face, where
the light is coming from.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:23.340
Every once in a while you
get weird stuff like this.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.630
And it all comes back to the fact

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:27.933
that it's software making choices.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.650
And the other half of that is skin tones.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.090
So you've heard me say
for a few years in a row

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:36.540
that I mostly prefer photos
coming from the Pixel's camera,

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:37.800
and we've done lots of tests

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.450
where I have me as a sample photo

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:40.770
and you can tell it looks really good.

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.930
Turns out Google's done this
thing over the past few years

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.360
with the Pixel camera called Real Tone.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:46.860
It doesn't get that much attention,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.130
but it turns out to be making
a pretty big difference here.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:53.700
Historically, a real issue for
film cameras back in the day

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.850
was that they were calibrated
for lighter skin tones

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:58.530
and people with darker skin tones

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:01.560
would typically be
underexposed in those pictures.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.010
So now fast forward today,
cameras are all software.

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:06.210
Smartphone cameras are software

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:07.620
so they can all make adjustments

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.280
to account for different variety
of skin tones, of course.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.790
But they still all do it to
different varying degrees.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:15.720
Like you might have noticed

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.700
a lot of phones sold in China

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.550
will just brighten up
faces across the board

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.410
because that's what people prefer

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.750
in photos in that region very often.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:26.220
Google goes the extra mile

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:28.590
to train their camera
software on data sets

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:30.510
that have a large variety of skin tones

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.180
to try to represent them
correctly across the board.

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:35.250
And that's what it's calling Real Tone.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.890
And Apple's cameras,
from what I've observed,

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:41.370
simply just like to evenly
light faces across the board

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.170
and doesn't necessarily account

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:47.370
for different white balances
and exposures necessary

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.580
to accurately represent
different types of skin tones

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:51.780
when I think they totally could.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:54.000
So basically, it turns
out this is a big part

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:57.180
of what we were observing in Pixel's

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.013
and a lot of the phones

00:10:58.013 --> 00:11:00.300
that do accurately represent my skin tone

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:04.200
finishing higher in this
blind voting thing that we did

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.060
because they happen to
do that really well.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.530
And that's a thing that
people really considered

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:08.610
when they voted on them.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:09.540
I haven't said this a lot,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.400
but I think this is one
of the earliest reasons

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.220
that I actually really
liked RED cameras was,

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.810
you know, obviously 8K is great,

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.660
Color Science is great,
but the way it represents

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:22.980
and renders my skin tone
accurately over a lot of,

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.750
you know, the Sonys and the ARRIs

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:26.220
and Canons that I've tried,

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:27.750
that's actually one of the things

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:29.100
that really drew me to these cameras.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.250
So all this software stuff
is why photo comparisons

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.830
between modern smartphones is so hard.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.510
Like there are a lot of channels
that do a really good job

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.420
with the side-by-side
photo test, you know,

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.820
but even as you're trying to
like pick one over the other,

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:42.930
you've probably noticed this,

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:44.400
you might like the way one of them

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.710
renders landscape photos over the other

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.350
but the way a different one renders photos

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.210
with your own skin tone and then the way

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:53.880
a different one renders photos
of your pet, for example.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.090
So I'm sure Apple will defend
everything they're doing now

00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:00.120
with their current cameras
as they typically do.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.340
But I'm gonna keep an
eye on what I'm also sure

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.690
which is they're for sure working

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.420
on tuning these new
cameras, dialing it in,

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.400
and eventually getting it better

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.100
with the iPhone 15 and 15 Pro.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:13.200
So back to the original question

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.190
from the beginning of the video,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:15.937
we can't leave that unanswered, which is,

00:12:15.937 --> 00:12:18.030
"All right, the Pixel 6a,

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.950
you like the Pixel photos, Marques,

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:22.920
it won the blind scientific camera test

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:24.090
but you still gave the trophy

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:27.390
for best overall camera
system to the iPhone,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.670
the very 14 Pro that
we've been talking about

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:31.380
this whole video, why?"

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:33.960
And if you listen carefully,
you already got it,

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:37.140
which is that scientific test that we did

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.300
tested one specific thing,

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.850
it tested the the small
postage-stamp-sized,

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:43.770
you know, exposure and
colors general thing

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:45.660
with a bunch of different factors,

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.210
but sharpness and detail

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:51.210
with all the compression
that we did wasn't tested.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.220
Also, speed of autofocus,

00:12:53.220 --> 00:12:55.920
reliability of autofocus wasn't tested.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.100
The open-close time of the camera app,

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.060
how fast and reliable you can
get a shot, wasn't tested.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:05.190
And also video wasn't tested.

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.770
So the microphone quality, video quality,

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.960
speed and reliability of autofocus there,

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:14.070
file formats, sharpness, HDR,
all that stuff, wasn't tested.

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:16.860
Maybe someday we will test
all that, but until then,

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:20.760
the lesson learned is the pretty pictures

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:24.370
that come from the Pixel or
whatever phone's in your pocket

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:30.290
are partly photons, but
primarily processing.

00:13:30.489 --> 00:13:31.500
(relaxed music)

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:32.910
Thanks for watching.

00:13:32.910 --> 00:13:34.260
Catch you guys the next one.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:35.093
Peace.

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:39.053
(record crackling)

